<section style="padding-top:40px">
  <div class="wrap" style="max-width:820px">
    <div class="guide-toc">
      <h4>In this guide</h4>
      <a href="#question">Start with the purpose of the session</a>
      <a href="#queue">Queue or pegboard rotation</a>
      <a href="#winner">Winner-stays-on / king of the court</a>
      <a href="#round">Round robin</a>
      <a href="#balanced">Balanced live rotation</a>
      <a href="#hybrid">Hybrid formats often work best</a>
      <a href="#table">Comparison table</a>
      <a href="#choose">How to choose</a>
      <a href="#faq">Frequently asked questions</a>
    </div>

    <div class="prose" style="margin-top:34px">
      <p class="lead"><strong>A club-night format is a policy for allocating a scarce resource: court time.</strong> Everything else - queues, pegboards, ratings, winners staying on, organiser picks - decides who gets that resource next and with whom.</p>

      <p>That is why arguments about the "best" format go nowhere. A competitive ladder wants winners to be rewarded. A social club may want the exact opposite: yesterday's result should have no effect on whether you get your fair share tonight. A tournament wants a complete fixture. A drop-in session wants to survive three late arrivals without redrawing anything.</p>

      <h2 id="question">Start with the purpose of the session</h2>
      <p>Before choosing mechanics, decide what the night is trying to optimise.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Equal opportunity to play:</strong> waits should be broadly comparable.</li>
        <li><strong>Competitive progression:</strong> winning should move you up or keep you on.</li>
        <li><strong>Good games:</strong> four players should be close enough in standard to produce a contest.</li>
        <li><strong>Mixing:</strong> partners and opponents should change across the evening.</li>
        <li><strong>Player autonomy:</strong> members should have some say over whom they play.</li>
        <li><strong>Low administration:</strong> the system should keep moving without a full-time organiser.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>You can have several of these. You cannot maximise all of them at once.</p>

      <h2 id="queue">Queue or pegboard rotation</h2>
      <p>This is the ordinary club workhorse: players who come off join a queue, and the next eligible players go on when a court becomes free. A physical pegboard is one implementation; a written list or digital queue is another.</p>

      <p><strong>Its great strength is resilience.</strong> Someone arrives at 8:17? Add them. Someone leaves at nine? Remove them. Court 3 becomes unavailable? The queue still exists. No fixture needs rebuilding.</p>

      <p>The queue itself, however, does not decide what makes a good game. Clubs solve that in different ways:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>take the next four strictly in order;</li>
        <li>let the person at the front choose three others;</li>
        <li>let an organiser choose from the next few;</li>
        <li>separate players loosely by standard;</li>
        <li>or use software to choose four while respecting waiting.</li>
      </ul>

      <p>That distinction matters. A queue can be excellent at <em>when</em> somebody plays and poor at <em>who</em> they play with.</p>

      <p><strong>Best for:</strong> ordinary drop-in club nights where attendance changes through the evening.</p>
      <p><strong>Main risk:</strong> hidden human bias in selecting partners, or mechanically taking the next four even when the resulting game is obviously poor.</p>

      <h2 id="winner">Winner-stays-on / king of the court</h2>
      <p>Winner-stays-on turns court allocation into a reward. The winners remain; the losers leave; challengers come on.</p>

      <p>That can be great fun. It creates consequence, momentum and a clear target: knock the current pair off. With short games and reasonably matched players it is an excellent competitive exercise.</p>

      <p>But its court-time distribution is not an accidental side effect. <strong>Better-performing players receive more play by design.</strong> If one pair wins six short games, they may be on court while several others cycle through one game each.</p>

      <p>That is not "unfair" if everybody knowingly joined a king-of-the-court session. It becomes a mismatch when a social club says everyone pays the same fee for broadly equal badminton and then uses winning to determine access to the courts.</p>

      <p>There are variants that soften the effect: winners stay for a maximum of two games; winning pair splits; both pairs leave after a fixed number of rallies; or courts are graded so challengers meet similar standards.</p>

      <p><strong>Best for:</strong> a competitive block, drill, ladder-style session or group of similar-standard players.</p>
      <p><strong>Main risk:</strong> turning playing strength into a court-time entitlement.</p>

      <h2 id="round">Round robin</h2>
      <p>A round robin answers a different question: how do we give a known group a complete and comprehensible set of fixtures?</p>

      <p>For a fixed event it is hard to beat. Everybody knows whom they will play, results can produce a table, and fixtures can be arranged so rest is distributed sensibly.</p>

      <p>Its weakness on an ordinary club night is not fairness but assumptions. A draw assumes the entrants are known. Club attendance often is not.</p>

      <p>A player arriving twenty minutes late can invalidate a neat sequence. A no-show creates byes. Somebody leaving with a sore calf can strand later fixtures. You can repair all of that, but by then you are manually running a tournament every Tuesday.</p>

      <p>Round robin also does not inherently mean balanced games. "Everyone plays everyone" deliberately creates some mismatches when ability varies; that may be fine because completeness is the objective.</p>

      <p><strong>Best for:</strong> internal competitions, graded groups, fixed attendance and events where results matter.</p>
      <p><strong>Main risk:</strong> rigidity when the participant list changes.</p>

      <h2 id="balanced">Balanced live rotation</h2>
      <p>A balanced live rotation keeps the flexibility of a queue but makes game composition another explicit decision.</p>

      <p>When a court becomes available, the system asks something like:</p>
      <p><em>Who has waited long enough to deserve consideration, and among those players, which four make a useful game without repeating the same partnerships?</em></p>

      <p>This is not one formal badminton format. It is a family of policies. One club may make waiting time almost absolute; another may allow a player to wait slightly longer for a much better match; another may force the first player in the queue into every next pick.</p>

      <p>The advantage is that fairness can be defined across several dimensions:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>court time;</li>
        <li>waiting time;</li>
        <li>standard of game;</li>
        <li>partner variety;</li>
        <li>opponent variety.</li>
      </ul>

      <p>The disadvantage is cognitive load. A good organiser can do this for ten players almost without thinking. At twenty-four players, four courts and a stream of late arrivals, remembering everybody's last three partners and cumulative wait becomes a real scheduling problem.</p>

      <p>Software such as <a href="/features/club-nights">ePegboard</a> is useful here because it can keep those histories without turning the organiser into an accountant. But automation does not remove the policy choice: the club still needs to decide how strongly fairness, game balance and queue order should compete.</p>

      <p><strong>Best for:</strong> ongoing social or mixed-standard nights where people arrive and leave dynamically.</p>
      <p><strong>Main risk:</strong> making the algorithm opaque or over-optimising game quality at the expense of understandable queue fairness.</p>

      <h2 id="hybrid">Hybrid formats often work best</h2>
      <p>Real clubs do not have to swear allegiance to one system.</p>

      <p>A normal Tuesday might use balanced rotation. The final half-hour could become king of the court for anyone who wants it. Once a month, sixteen regulars might enter a round robin. A beginner court might use coach-arranged games while the rest of the hall runs from a queue.</p>

      <p>That is not inconsistency. Different parts of the programme have different objectives.</p>

      <p>The useful discipline is to label the change. If winner-stays-on begins at 9:30, tell people that the fairness rule has changed for the final half-hour. Players are far more accepting of a trade-off when it is explicit.</p>

      <h2 id="table">Comparison table</h2>
      <div class="cmp-wrap">
        <table class="cmp-table">
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Format</th><th>Handles drop-ins</th><th>Court-time equality</th><th>Game balance</th><th>Admin</th><th>Best use</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td><strong>Queue / pegboard</strong></td><td>Excellent</td><td>Good if queue rules are consistent</td><td>Depends how four are chosen</td><td>Low</td><td>General club night</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Winner-stays-on</strong></td><td>Good</td><td>Low by design</td><td>Can self-sort over time</td><td>Very low</td><td>Competitive block / drill</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Round robin</strong></td><td>Poor</td><td>Predictable within the draw</td><td>Not guaranteed</td><td>Low after setup, high when disrupted</td><td>Fixed event</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Balanced live rotation</strong></td><td>Excellent</td><td>Configurable</td><td>Strong if ratings are useful</td><td>High by hand; low with software</td><td>Mixed social club</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <h2 id="choose">How to choose</h2>
      <p>For most ordinary club nights, start with a queue because it matches the reality of changing attendance. Then decide how clever the game-selection step needs to be.</p>

      <p>If your players are close in standard and happily self-select, a physical pegboard may be enough. If the room is mixed and organisers spend half the night fixing mismatches, add balancing. If people complain about repetitive partnerships, track variety. If the group explicitly wants competitive consequences, introduce winner-stays-on or a ladder as a separate format.</p>

      <div class="guide-callout">
        <h4>The useful question</h4>
        <p style="margin-top:8px"><strong>What behaviour does this format reward?</strong> Winner-stays-on rewards winning. A strict queue rewards waiting. A round robin rewards completion. Balanced rotation tries to reward patience while improving the game itself. Choose the behaviour your club wants.</p>
      </div>

      <h2 id="faq">Frequently asked questions</h2>
      @foreach (var f in faqPairs)
      {
        <h3>@f.Q</h3>
        <p>@f.A</p>
      }

      <p>For the mechanics of live rotation, see <a href="/guides/badminton-club-night-rotation">badminton club-night rotation</a>. For a fixed event, see <a href="/guides/how-to-run-a-badminton-round-robin">how to run a badminton round robin</a>.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
